1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:01,000
improving actions.

2
00:00:01,000 --> 00:00:05,920
It does mean that if you require that every action be a Pareto improvement, then we couldn't

3
00:00:05,920 --> 00:00:06,920
do anything.

4
00:00:06,920 --> 00:00:09,920
But someone might object to that.

5
00:00:09,920 --> 00:00:14,560
Or you might say, well, so what do we define as the status quo?

6
00:00:14,560 --> 00:00:15,560
Wherever we are.

7
00:00:15,560 --> 00:00:16,560
All right.

8
00:00:16,560 --> 00:00:25,480
So the standard of Pareto efficiency combined with the related concepts of Pareto improvements.

9
00:00:25,480 --> 00:00:27,240
And remember, so key things.

10
00:00:27,240 --> 00:00:33,040
So when, if there are highly stylized, two people on a desert island and one of them

11
00:00:33,040 --> 00:00:34,040
buys something for someone else.

12
00:00:34,040 --> 00:00:35,040
Is that a Pareto improvement?

13
00:00:35,040 --> 00:00:36,040
Yeah, that qualifies.

14
00:00:36,040 --> 00:00:39,520
There's nobody else around on earth to be affected.

15
00:00:39,520 --> 00:00:42,200
So you have your Pareto improvement there.

16
00:00:42,200 --> 00:00:43,200
All right.

17
00:00:43,200 --> 00:00:52,200
So, but, so, so is the fail, so when you take a look at this, so you see two people, when

18
00:00:52,200 --> 00:00:57,720
you see two people trading, is it correct to say that the trade is an example of Pareto

19
00:00:57,720 --> 00:00:58,720
efficiency?

20
00:00:58,720 --> 00:01:03,800
That the trade moves you to Pareto efficiency via a Pareto improvement.

21
00:01:03,800 --> 00:01:08,280
And the key point is, if something, if you have a Pareto, the only way you can have a

22
00:01:08,280 --> 00:01:11,240
Pareto improvement is if you're not Pareto efficient yet.

23
00:01:11,240 --> 00:01:14,880
Because once you reach Pareto efficiency, then there can no longer be any further Pareto

24
00:01:14,880 --> 00:01:15,880
improvements.

25
00:01:15,880 --> 00:01:16,880
All right.

26
00:01:16,880 --> 00:01:17,880
We'll come back to this, but we're pointing out.

27
00:01:17,880 --> 00:01:18,880
Okay.

28
00:01:18,880 --> 00:01:19,880
All right.

29
00:01:19,880 --> 00:01:22,000
So we have this concept of Pareto efficiency.

30
00:01:22,000 --> 00:01:23,600
It has some uses.

31
00:01:23,600 --> 00:01:26,960
You probably have done some homework problems and some exam problems involving it.

32
00:01:26,960 --> 00:01:29,840
You may have read some papers involving it.

33
00:01:29,840 --> 00:01:35,240
But it is way less useful in the real world than most economists would like you to believe.

34
00:01:35,240 --> 00:01:36,240
Right.

35
00:01:36,240 --> 00:01:40,200
In fact, for real world purposes, unless we get down to a really small population, if

36
00:01:40,200 --> 00:01:43,680
you're going to like two people on an island, so they have the entire, if the human population

37
00:01:43,680 --> 00:01:48,320
dwindles to two, suddenly this becomes a really relevant concept to you.

38
00:01:48,320 --> 00:01:52,280
But as long as we know, although even there, what if it's like that North Korean fan from

39
00:01:52,280 --> 00:01:53,280
Australia who's the other person?

40
00:01:53,280 --> 00:01:54,280
Me and that guy.

41
00:01:54,280 --> 00:01:55,280
All right.

42
00:01:55,280 --> 00:01:56,280
It's a little scary.

43
00:01:56,280 --> 00:01:57,280
Me and the guy who wants my watch to explode.

44
00:01:57,280 --> 00:01:58,280
Hopefully when you get to know each other, some of the hate will diminish, but there

45
00:01:58,280 --> 00:01:59,280
are no guarantees.

46
00:01:59,280 --> 00:02:00,280
All right.

47
00:02:00,280 --> 00:02:17,720
So in any case, in the real world, when economists analyze any actual policy, they normally forget

48
00:02:17,720 --> 00:02:23,400
about credit efficiency, forget about credit improvements, and switch to this one here.

49
00:02:23,400 --> 00:02:28,480
Counter-efficiency, also known as cost-benefit efficiency.

50
00:02:28,480 --> 00:02:34,160
So a situation is counter-efficient if and only if the dollar value of social resources

51
00:02:34,160 --> 00:02:35,160
is maximized.

52
00:02:35,160 --> 00:02:40,160
Of course, it could also be the pound value or the yen value or the euro value or whatever.

53
00:02:40,160 --> 00:02:44,280
Then a counter-efficient improvement is any change that raises the dollar value of social

54
00:02:44,280 --> 00:02:45,280
resources.

55
00:02:45,280 --> 00:02:48,920
So this is the definition of the concept.

56
00:02:48,920 --> 00:02:49,920
All right.

57
00:02:49,920 --> 00:02:53,640
Now, every counter-efficient situation is frame-efficient.

58
00:02:53,640 --> 00:03:00,280
So if we have maximized the dollar value of social resources, this automatically means

59
00:03:00,280 --> 00:03:04,280
that the resources are in the hands of whoever puts the highest dollar value on them.

60
00:03:04,280 --> 00:03:08,480
And if resources are in the hands of the person who puts the highest dollar value on them,

61
00:03:08,480 --> 00:03:14,240
is there any way that you can go and move resources to somebody else and get a rate

62
00:03:14,240 --> 00:03:15,240
improvement?

63
00:03:15,240 --> 00:03:16,240
No.

64
00:03:16,240 --> 00:03:21,360
Because remember, the whole idea of trade is that people who put lower dollar values

65
00:03:21,360 --> 00:03:25,640
on things sell them to people who put higher dollar values on things.

66
00:03:25,640 --> 00:03:30,440
So if we already are at the maximum dollar value of everything, then everything is in

67
00:03:30,440 --> 00:03:34,320
the hands of the person who puts the highest dollar value on it, and so there's no room

68
00:03:34,320 --> 00:03:38,920
for them to sell to anybody else because anybody else would put a lower dollar value on it.

69
00:03:38,920 --> 00:03:42,160
So every counter-efficient situation is frame-efficient.

70
00:03:42,160 --> 00:03:45,000
But most prey-efficient situations are not counter-efficient.

71
00:03:45,000 --> 00:03:46,000
All right.

72
00:03:46,000 --> 00:03:48,520
So let's go back to the watch example.

73
00:03:48,520 --> 00:03:51,280
So suppose that you value a watch at $20.

74
00:03:51,280 --> 00:03:53,560
I value it at $30.

75
00:03:53,560 --> 00:03:56,520
All the strangers you will ever encounter who ask you the time value you're having it

76
00:03:56,520 --> 00:03:57,880
at 10 cents.

77
00:03:57,880 --> 00:04:01,880
All the strangers who value me having the watch that I will ever encounter value my

78
00:04:01,880 --> 00:04:02,880
having it at 10 cents.

79
00:04:02,880 --> 00:04:05,880
But of course, those are different strangers.

80
00:04:05,880 --> 00:04:06,880
Remember that.

81
00:04:06,880 --> 00:04:12,640
Now, if I have the watch, the situation is counter-efficient and prey-efficient because

82
00:04:12,640 --> 00:04:18,400
the dollar value of the social value is $30.10 when I have it, $20.10 when you have it.

83
00:04:18,400 --> 00:04:22,960
So it's prey-efficient, of course, because there's no way to make any one person better

84
00:04:22,960 --> 00:04:25,280
off without making someone else worse off.

85
00:04:25,280 --> 00:04:29,000
But it's also counter-efficient because we've maximized dollar value.

86
00:04:29,000 --> 00:04:32,360
On the other hand, if you have the watch, then it is prey-efficient for you to keep

87
00:04:32,360 --> 00:04:37,120
the watch because if you sold it to me, you would hurt those people that ask you the time.

88
00:04:37,120 --> 00:04:40,240
But it is not counter-efficient for you to keep the watch because the social value of

89
00:04:40,240 --> 00:04:45,600
you owning the watch is only $20.10, whereas when I own the watch, it's $30.10.

90
00:04:45,600 --> 00:04:47,360
So that's the idea.

91
00:04:47,360 --> 00:04:50,400
Now, similarly, every Pareto improvement is a Calrix improvement.

92
00:04:50,400 --> 00:04:54,600
Because if you can find a Pareto improvement, this means that resources are moving from

93
00:04:54,600 --> 00:04:58,880
people who value them less in dollar terms to people who value them more.

94
00:04:58,880 --> 00:05:03,120
And therefore, we have a, that is a Pareto improvement, automatically a Calrix improvement

95
00:05:03,120 --> 00:05:06,480
because the dollar value of social resources is going up.

96
00:05:06,480 --> 00:05:10,240
But most Calrix improvements are not Pareto improvements.

97
00:05:10,240 --> 00:05:14,840
Most trades are going to hurt somebody on earth in a way that they are not compensated

98
00:05:14,840 --> 00:05:15,840
for.

99
00:05:15,840 --> 00:05:19,000
And again, remember, there's 7 billion people on earth who can be affected negatively by

100
00:05:19,000 --> 00:05:21,280
whatever transaction you're proposing.

101
00:05:21,280 --> 00:05:27,200
So to be a real, to be a real Pareto improvement, zero of them can lose.

102
00:05:27,200 --> 00:05:29,640
Zero of them can lose.

103
00:05:29,640 --> 00:05:34,280
All right, but there are tons of Calrix improvements in the world.

104
00:05:34,280 --> 00:05:35,560
This is really easy.

105
00:05:35,560 --> 00:05:39,320
So if there's a whole bunch of people who lose, but they don't lose that much, and there's

106
00:05:39,320 --> 00:05:43,800
a whole bunch of people who gain and gain by more, then we've got our Calrix improvements.

107
00:05:43,800 --> 00:05:46,280
It's really easy to do.

108
00:05:46,280 --> 00:05:55,600
So if, say, if there are a hundred people benefit from something, ten people lose, and

109
00:05:55,600 --> 00:06:01,200
the dollar value they put on average is the same, then of course the Calrix improvement

110
00:06:01,200 --> 00:06:05,560
is to help the larger number of people.

111
00:06:05,560 --> 00:06:07,720
But what about compensating those ten?

112
00:06:07,720 --> 00:06:10,840
Calrix improvements don't require any compensation of any kind.

113
00:06:10,840 --> 00:06:14,320
It's just about maximizing the dollar value of social resources.

114
00:06:14,320 --> 00:06:20,680
So yes, if you can go and make me a billion dollars worse off, and then make the rest

115
00:06:20,680 --> 00:06:23,960
of the world a trillion dollars better off, that's a Calrix improvement.

116
00:06:23,960 --> 00:06:24,960
And what about me?

117
00:06:24,960 --> 00:06:25,960
Tough luck.

118
00:06:25,960 --> 00:06:26,960
Tough luck.

119
00:06:26,960 --> 00:06:30,240
That is built into the Calrix standard.

120
00:06:30,240 --> 00:06:31,240
Okay.

121
00:06:31,240 --> 00:06:48,120
Now Calrix efficiency is sometimes described as potential Pareto efficiency.

122
00:06:48,120 --> 00:06:49,120
Potential Pareto efficiency.

123
00:06:49,120 --> 00:06:50,120
All right.

124
00:06:50,120 --> 00:06:56,360
And the idea is that if the value of social resources rises, then assuming perfect continuity

125
00:06:56,360 --> 00:07:02,600
and no transactions costs, assuming those two things, then you could compensate all

126
00:07:02,600 --> 00:07:04,400
the losers by sharing the gains and surplus.

127
00:07:04,400 --> 00:07:09,800
So in other words, given two totally false assumptions, then it would be a Pareto improvement.

128
00:07:09,800 --> 00:07:10,800
All right.

129
00:07:10,800 --> 00:07:11,800
So I get it.

130
00:07:11,800 --> 00:07:20,560
It would make more sense to say that it's Pareto efficiency with two lies.

131
00:07:20,560 --> 00:07:24,680
That would make a lot more, that would be a much more honest description of it.

132
00:07:24,680 --> 00:07:30,000
But people like the idea, especially pure theorists are in love with the idea of Pareto

133
00:07:30,000 --> 00:07:31,000
efficiency.

134
00:07:31,000 --> 00:07:36,000
And of course, you can absolutely write down a theoretical model with perfect continuity,

135
00:07:36,000 --> 00:07:39,000
no transactions costs, and then they're right.

136
00:07:39,000 --> 00:07:43,400
But this is totally useless for analyzing anything in the real world.

137
00:07:43,400 --> 00:07:46,720
So what this could mean, essentially you could, if transactions costs ranging compensation

138
00:07:46,720 --> 00:07:52,240
were zero, and in the real world, when is ranging compensation free of all transaction

139
00:07:52,240 --> 00:07:53,240
costs?

140
00:07:53,240 --> 00:07:54,240
Never.

141
00:07:54,240 --> 00:07:58,640
So for all policy purposes, it's irrelevant.

142
00:07:58,640 --> 00:07:59,640
All right.

143
00:07:59,640 --> 00:08:01,200
Now this bothers a lot of people.

144
00:08:01,200 --> 00:08:06,080
And again, there are many Austrians who have a philosophical objection to the idea of Calrix

145
00:08:06,080 --> 00:08:07,080
efficiency.

146
00:08:07,080 --> 00:08:11,680
And again, what people sometimes say is, well, aren't transactions just as real as any other

147
00:08:11,680 --> 00:08:13,360
cost?

148
00:08:13,360 --> 00:08:19,080
And the Calrix answer is, of course, they're every bit as real as other costs.

149
00:08:19,080 --> 00:08:23,080
And that's why sometimes we don't have a transaction.

150
00:08:23,080 --> 00:08:27,640
So the Calrix answer, in other words, is to say, look, well, we thought about whether

151
00:08:27,640 --> 00:08:30,400
going and doing a change with compensation would be a Calrix improvement.

152
00:08:30,400 --> 00:08:31,440
And you know what?

153
00:08:31,440 --> 00:08:33,880
It wasn't because it was a pain in the neck to do the compensation.

154
00:08:33,880 --> 00:08:38,000
And then we thought about whether doing a change without any compensation at all would

155
00:08:38,000 --> 00:08:39,000
be a Calrix improvement.

156
00:08:39,000 --> 00:08:40,000
And you know what?

157
00:08:40,000 --> 00:08:41,000
It was.

158
00:08:41,000 --> 00:08:42,000
Awesome.

159
00:08:42,000 --> 00:08:43,000
All right.

160
00:08:43,000 --> 00:08:45,480
So for example, just abolishing rent control, right?

161
00:08:45,480 --> 00:08:48,440
Is that going to abolish rent control without compensation?

162
00:08:48,440 --> 00:08:52,880
Is that likely to be, according to stuff that you've read in Econ 1, is that likely to be

163
00:08:52,880 --> 00:08:53,880
a Calrix improvement?

164
00:08:53,880 --> 00:08:59,200
Sure, because it goes and moves housing into the hands of people of value more away from

165
00:08:59,200 --> 00:09:00,200
those who handle it less.

166
00:09:00,200 --> 00:09:01,200
All right.

167
00:09:01,200 --> 00:09:07,920
So how about abolishing rent control with an elaborate system of compensation that specifies

168
00:09:07,920 --> 00:09:11,080
where we're going to go and we're going to raise tax on landlords, and then we're going

169
00:09:11,080 --> 00:09:13,600
to compensate people based upon the number of years they have, and we're going to come

170
00:09:13,600 --> 00:09:18,320
up with a giant rule book of 1,000 pages based upon who gets compensation and who doesn't.

171
00:09:18,320 --> 00:09:21,680
Because remember, you might want to compensate people that would have moved into the apartments

172
00:09:21,680 --> 00:09:24,120
but haven't moved to New York City yet, for example.

173
00:09:24,120 --> 00:09:25,120
What about them?

174
00:09:25,120 --> 00:09:26,520
You get rid of rent control.

175
00:09:26,520 --> 00:09:29,640
Has anybody ever thought about the people who would have gotten a rent control apartment

176
00:09:29,640 --> 00:09:31,640
after the current person dies?

177
00:09:31,640 --> 00:09:32,640
What about them?

178
00:09:32,640 --> 00:09:33,960
Who are they?

179
00:09:33,960 --> 00:09:34,960
Where are they?

180
00:09:34,960 --> 00:09:38,200
They could be living in Kansas in the middle of nowhere right now.

181
00:09:38,200 --> 00:09:41,920
They themselves will never move to New York unless they can find rent control apartments.

182
00:09:41,920 --> 00:09:43,160
All right.

183
00:09:43,160 --> 00:09:47,600
So something like that is much less likely to actually be a Calrix improvement because

184
00:09:47,600 --> 00:09:51,400
it comes with so many strings attached, it's really hard to actually make it fly.

185
00:09:51,400 --> 00:09:52,400
All right.

186
00:09:52,400 --> 00:09:58,320
Whereas the simple cut the cord, tough luck, in many ways that is more likely to actually

187
00:09:58,320 --> 00:09:59,720
be a Calrix improvement.

188
00:09:59,720 --> 00:10:00,720
Right.

189
00:10:00,720 --> 00:10:04,400
And just think about it this way.

190
00:10:04,400 --> 00:10:10,960
Anytime you think about how the cost of arranging compensation, the Calrix answer is often,

191
00:10:10,960 --> 00:10:15,360
though it doesn't have to be, let's see, how will I deal with all the difficulties

192
00:10:15,360 --> 00:10:18,560
with arranging compensation for people?

193
00:10:18,560 --> 00:10:21,400
How about no compensation for anyone?

194
00:10:21,400 --> 00:10:22,400
That's my solution.

195
00:10:22,400 --> 00:10:23,400
All right.

196
00:10:23,400 --> 00:10:25,920
And that is one way you can do it.

197
00:10:25,920 --> 00:10:26,920
Right.

198
00:10:26,920 --> 00:10:27,920
And it's so complicated.

199
00:10:27,920 --> 00:10:30,160
It's not complicated if we don't do it.

200
00:10:30,160 --> 00:10:31,160
Right.

201
00:10:31,160 --> 00:10:38,040
And if you look at the real world, how often do we really make any policy changes, any

202
00:10:38,040 --> 00:10:42,040
real effort made to remotely compensate all the losers?

203
00:10:42,040 --> 00:10:43,040
No.

204
00:10:43,040 --> 00:10:48,080
Sometimes, and usually not even there, sometimes we only find some really loudmouth group of

205
00:10:48,080 --> 00:10:50,960
losers and we compensate them.

206
00:10:50,960 --> 00:10:56,480
So for example, there are a lot of laws that say that if you have lost your job as a result

207
00:10:56,480 --> 00:10:59,520
of international trade, then you get government job training.

208
00:10:59,520 --> 00:11:01,040
All right.

209
00:11:01,040 --> 00:11:06,760
But this only applies to people who are in the very clear cut export competing industries.

210
00:11:06,760 --> 00:11:11,520
We don't worry about people who lost their job because they were competing with a product

211
00:11:11,520 --> 00:11:16,560
where an import was an input, which then reduced the prices, which then indirectly caused them

212
00:11:16,560 --> 00:11:17,680
to lose their job.

213
00:11:17,680 --> 00:11:22,360
We don't worry about people that have lower real estate values because the industries

214
00:11:22,360 --> 00:11:26,600
in their area that they didn't actually work for were hurt by import competition.

215
00:11:26,600 --> 00:11:29,560
All these different groups of losers, what do we normally say to them?

216
00:11:29,560 --> 00:11:31,560
Well normally we just pretend they don't even exist.

217
00:11:31,560 --> 00:11:32,560
Right.

218
00:11:32,560 --> 00:11:33,560
Tough luck.

219
00:11:33,560 --> 00:11:34,560
And if they do come up, tough luck.

220
00:11:34,560 --> 00:11:35,560
All right.

221
00:11:35,560 --> 00:11:40,440
So, or if you think about, you know, like what's generally done in countries when they

222
00:11:40,440 --> 00:11:44,560
privatize resources, they might find a few people that are losing and try to give them

223
00:11:44,560 --> 00:11:45,560
something.

224
00:11:45,560 --> 00:11:47,560
But most people, well, just going to have to get used to it.

225
00:11:47,560 --> 00:11:48,560
Too bad.

226
00:11:48,560 --> 00:11:49,560
Too bad.

227
00:11:49,560 --> 00:11:50,560
All right.

228
00:11:50,560 --> 00:11:52,560
Okay.

229
00:11:52,560 --> 00:11:53,560
All right.

230
00:11:53,560 --> 00:11:58,240
So, a common complaint that, you know, that transaction costs are just as real as any

231
00:11:58,240 --> 00:12:03,400
other cost, they absolutely are, which is why if you are analyzing CalREx efficiency,

232
00:12:03,400 --> 00:12:07,360
you or something is a CalREx improvement, you say, all right, so what compensation is

233
00:12:07,360 --> 00:12:11,600
in there, what transaction costs are in there, and then we go and we count that.

234
00:12:11,600 --> 00:12:14,400
But we don't have to count transactions that don't happen because we're not bothering

235
00:12:14,400 --> 00:12:16,480
to have the transaction.

236
00:12:16,480 --> 00:12:17,480
That's the idea.

237
00:12:17,480 --> 00:12:18,480
Right.

238
00:12:18,480 --> 00:12:22,600
So, you know, another example would be we could either have a big, we could have a big

239
00:12:22,600 --> 00:12:26,720
negotiation about when the midterm is going to be, which would then, of course, burn up

240
00:12:26,720 --> 00:12:30,120
a whole lot of transactions costs as we go and talk about it and argue and get mad at

241
00:12:30,120 --> 00:12:32,600
each other and everything else and lobby.

242
00:12:32,600 --> 00:12:37,080
Or we could just have a dictator decrees when the midterm will be.

243
00:12:37,080 --> 00:12:38,080
Okay.

244
00:12:38,080 --> 00:12:41,800
And if we're going and seeing whether one is a CalREx improvement or the other, we just

245
